From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 09:08:14 +0000
Subject: clk: intel: add CGU clock driver for Intel MIPS (GRX500) SoCs

Add a common-clock-framework driver for the Clock Generation Unit of
the Intel (formerly Lantiq) GRX500/xRX500 family: PLL0A/0B/3 read-out,
CPU/DDR/NGI dividers and muxes, fixed interface clocks and the
GSR1/GSR2 peripheral clock gates.

The provider is a CLK_OF_DECLARE one and hands out struct clk_hw
through of_clk_add_hw_provider(). plat_time_init() derives
mips_hpt_frequency from CLK_CPU before the platform bus exists, so the
CGU has to come up from of_clk_init(); the hw provider API is available
there, as drivers/clk/clk-eyeq.c shows.

One deliberate policy: every gate is registered CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED. The
boot chain leaves this SoC's peripheral gates in a working state and
the vendor driver never changes one -- its "initial gate default" write
is inert, because the mask is passed in the shift parameter with a zero
mask, and every vendor and reference board instead boots with
clk_ignore_unused on the kernel command line. Registering the gates
CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED is the same policy expressed in the driver, where it
does not depend on a command line the platform cannot control. The
gates the vendor declares as must-be-on are CBM, EBU, UART, PAE, GSWIP
and both USB controllers; the packet datapath does not survive losing
them.

Originally submitted for the 2018 intel-mips platform series by
Yixin Zhu <yixin.zhu@intel.com>:

  https://lore.kernel.org/all/20180803030237.3366-1-songjun.wu@linux.intel.com/

forward-ported to v6.18 (CLK_IS_BASIC removal, 'const static' cleanups,
the clk_hw provider API) with two corrections to the original: the
fractional-N PLL scale is BIT(21) for the 21-bit feedback-divider
fraction this part carries, not BIT(20), and the gate policy above.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

